: Learn more:\nL136: \nL137: cite18†Google DeepMind blog†deepmind.google cite19†Google Research blog†research.google cite20†Google Developers blog†developers.googleblog.com cite21†Google Cloud blog†cloud.google.com L138: \nL139:   * Products & platforms\nL140: ### Products & platforms\nL141: \nL142: See all in Products & platforms\nL143: \nL144:     * Products\nL145: \nL146:       * cite22†Search L147:       * cite23†Maps L148:       * cite24†Chrome L149:       * cite25†Google Health L150:       * cite26†Google Workspace L151:       * cite27†Learning & Education L152:       * cite28†Shopping L153:       * cite29†See all L154:     * Platforms\nL155: \nL156:       * cite30†Android L157:       * cite31†Google Play L158:       * cite32†Wear OS L159:       * cite33†See all L160:     * Devices\nL161:       * cite34†Pixel L162:       * cite35†Google Nest L163:       * cite36†Fitbit L164:       * cite37†Chromebook L165:       * cite38†Googlebook L166:       * cite39†See all L167: \nL168: Learn more:\nL169: \nL170: cite40†Google Ads & Commerce blog cite41†Waze blog L171: \nL172:   * Company news\nL173: ### Company news\nL174: \nL175: See all in Company news\nL176: \nL177:     * Outreach & initiatives\nL178: \nL179:       * cite42†Creating opportunity L180:       * cite15†Safety & security L181:       * cite43†Google.org L182:       * cite44†Public policy L183:       * cite45†Sustainability L184:       * cite16†Health L185:       * cite46†See all L186:     * Leadership\nL187: \nL188:       * cite47†Sundar Pichai, CEO L189:       * cite48†More authors L190:       * cite48†See all L191:     * Inside Google\nL192: \nL193:       * cite49†Around the globe L194:       * cite50†Life at Google L195:       * cite51†See all L196: \nL197: Learn more:\nL198: \nL199: cite52†Google Security blog L200: \nL201:   * cite1†Feed L202: Introducing Nano Banana Pro\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # Introducing Nano Banana Pro\nL215: \nL216: Nov 20, 2025\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: Turn your visions into studio-quality designs with unprecedented control, improved text rendering and enhanced world knowledge.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: Naina Raisinghani\nL225: \nL226: Product Manager, Google DeepMind\nL227: \nL228: * * *\nL229: \nL230: Read AI-generated summary\nL231: Google DeepMind introduces Nano Banana Pro, a new image generation and editing model built on Gemini 3 Pro. You can use it to create accurate visuals with legible text in multiple languages. Try Nano Banana Pro today across Google products like the Gemini app, Google Ads, and Google AI Studio.\nL232: \nL233: Summaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\nL234:   * \"Introducing Nano Banana Pro\" is all about Google's new state-of-the-art image generation and editing model.\nL235:   * Nano Banana Pro helps you visualize ideas, create infographics, and turn notes into diagrams with ease.\nL236:   * Generate images with accurate text in multiple languages, perfect for mockups, posters, and international content.\nL237:   * Create high-fidelity visuals with consistent branding, advanced creative controls, and up to 4K resolution.\nL238:   * You can try Nano Banana Pro in Gemini, Google Ads, Workspace, and more, with SynthID watermarks for transparency.\nL239: Summaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\nL240: #### Explore other styles:\nL241: \nL242:   * General summary\nL243:   * Bullet points\nL244: \nL245: Just a few months ago we released cite55†Nano Banana , our Gemini 2.5 Flash Image model. From cite56†restoring old photos†x.com to generating cite57†mini figurines†x.com , Nano Banana was a big step in image editing that empowered casual creators to express their creativity.\nL246: Today, we’re introducing cite58†Nano Banana Pro (Gemini 3 Pro Image)†deepmind.google , our new state-of-the art image generation and editing model. Built on cite59†Gemini 3 Pro , Nano Banana Pro uses Gemini’s state-of-the-art reasoning and real-world knowledge to visualize information better than ever before.\nL247: ## How Nano Banana Pro helps you bring any idea or design to life\nL248: \nL249: Nano Banana Pro can help you visualize any idea and design anything — from prototypes, to representing data as infographics, to turning handwritten notes into diagrams.\nL250: \nL251: With Nano Banana Pro, now you can:\nL252: \nL253: Generate more accurate, context-rich visuals based on enhanced reasoning, world knowledge and real-time information\nL254: With Gemini 3’s advanced reasoning, Nano Banana Pro doesn’t just create beautiful images, it also helps you create more helpful content. You can get accurate educational explainers to learn more about a new subject, like context-rich infographics and diagrams based on the content you provide or facts from the real world. Nano Banana Pro can also connect to Google Search's vast knowledge base to help you create a quick snapshot for a recipe or visualize real-time information like weather or sports.\nL255: cite60†Image: Infographic about plants using nano banana pro†storage.googleapis.com L256: \nL257: An infographic of the common house plant, String of Turtles, with information on origins, care essentials and growth patterns.\nL258: \nL259: Prompt: Create an infographic about this plant focusing on interesting information.\nL260: \nL261: cite61†Image: Infographic about making chai made using Nano Banana Pro†storage.googleapis.com L262: Step-by-step infographic for making Elaichi Chai (cardamom tea), demonstrating the ability to visualize recipes and real-world information.\nL263: \nL264: Prompt: Create an infographic that shows how to make elaichi chai\nL265: \nL266: cite62†Image: Comic book style weather infographic using Nano Banana Pro†storage.googleapis.com L267: \nL268: We used Nano Banana Pro to pull in real-time weather via Search grounding to build a pop-art infographic.\nL269: \nL270: Generate better visuals with more accurate, legible text directly in the image in multiple languages\nL271: Nano Banana Pro is the best model for creating images with correctly rendered and legible text directly in the image, whether you’re looking for a short tagline, or a long paragraph. Gemini 3 is great at understanding depth and nuance, which unlocks a world of possibilities with image editing and generation — especially with text. Now cite63†you can create more detailed text in mockups or posters with a wider variety of textures, fonts and calligraphy.\nL272: With Gemini’s enhanced multilingual reasoning, you can generate text in multiple languages, or localize and translate your content so you can scale internationally and/or share content more easily with friends and family.\nL273: cite64†Image: Storyboard showing four panels for \"Urban Astronaut\": establishing shot, medium shot, close-up, and POV shot up city buildings.†storage.googleapis.com L274: \nL275: A black and white storyboard sketch showing an establishing shot, medium shot, close-up, and POV shot for a film scene.\nL276: \nL277: Prompt: Create a storyboard for this scene\nL278: \nL279: cite65†Image: Berlin street view with facades spelling \"BERLIN\" in blue, red, white, and black block letters.†storage.googleapis.com L280: The word 'BERLIN' integrated into the architecture of a city block, spanning across multiple buildings.\nL281: \nL282: Prompt: View of a cozy street in Berlin on a bright sunny day, stark shadows. the old houses are oddly shaped like letters that spell out \"BERLIN\" Colored in Blue, Red, White and black. The houses still look like houses and the resemblance to letters is subtle.\nL283: \nL284: cite66†Image: Crash, whoosh, shiver, bang, drip, squeeze, roar, wobble in stylized fonts†storage.googleapis.com L285: Calligraphy inspired by meaning, showcasing the ability to generate expressive text with a wider variety of textures and fonts.\nL286: \nL287: Prompt: make 8 minimalistic logos, each is an expressive word, and make letters convey a message or sound visually to express the meaning of this word in a dramatic way. composition: flat vector rendering of all logos in black on a single white background\nL288: \nL289: cite67†Image: Cans in English and Korean†storage.googleapis.com L290: A beverage campaign concept showcasing accurate translation and rendering of English text into Korean.\nL291: \nL292: Prompt: translate all the English text on the three yellow and blue cans into Korean, while keeping everything else the same\nL293: \nL294: cite68†Image: The word \"TYPOGRAPHY\" in bold, stacked blue and magenta sans-serif text with a halftone/distressed effect.†storage.googleapis.com L295: \nL296: A graphic design featuring the word 'TYPOGRAPHY' with a retro, screen-printed texture.\nL297: Prompt: A vibrant, eye-catching \"TYPOGRAPHY\" design on a textured off-white background. The letters are bold, blocky, extra condensed and create a 3D effect with overlapping layers of bright blue and hot pink, each with a halftone dot pattern, evoking a retro print aesthetic. 16:9 aspect ratio\nL298: \nL299: cite69†Image: \"How much wood would a woodchuck chuck if a woodchuck could chuck wood\" made out of wood chucked by a woodchuck.†storage.googleapis.com L300: Blending text and texture in a creative way by integrating the phrase into a woodchopping scene.\nL301: \nL302: Prompt: Create an image showing the phrase \"How much wood would a woodchuck chuck if a woodchuck could chuck wood\" made out of wood chucked by a woodchuck.\nL303: \nL304: Create high-fidelity visuals with upgraded creative capabilities\nL305:   * Consistency by design: With Nano Banana Pro, you can blend more elements than ever before, using up to 14 images and maintaining the consistency and resemblance of up to 5 people. Whether turning sketches into products or blueprints into photorealistic 3D structures, you can now bridge the gap between concept and creation. Apply your desired visual look and feel to your mockups with ease, ensuring your branding remains seamless and consistent across every touchpoint.\nL306: cite70†Image: Fluffy characters watching TV†storage.googleapis.com L307: \nL308: Maintaining the consistency of up to 14 inputs, including multiple characters, across a complex composition.\nL309: Prompt: A medium shot of the 14 fluffy characters sitting squeezed together side-by-side on a worn beige fabric sofa and on the floor. They are all facing forwards, watching a vintage, wooden-boxed television set placed on a low wooden table in front of the sofa. The room is dimly lit, with warm light from a window on the left and the glow from the TV illuminating the creatures' faces and fluffy textures.\n"